  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 302 PM CST Thu Nov 5 2026\n\nAt the synoptic scale, our weather pattern looks to remain \nrelatively benign. A cold front trailing a surface low over the \nGreat Lakes has continued pushing southward, bringing cooler \ntemperatures in the 50s and low, dense cloud cover for northern \nMO/northeastern KS. The positively tilted mid-level trough over the \nGreat Lakes has continued to progress eastward, with mid-level \nridging building in behind the retreating trough. As the axis of the \ntrough remains slanted over central MO, continued chances for \ndrizzle/light rain persist into tonight with weak ascent from \nvorticity maximums within the atmospheric flow interacting with \nsaturation in the low 0-1 km of the atmosphere. With overnight lows \nin the low/mid 40s and high 30s, though, any impacts from this \ndrizzle will remain minimal. Chances (10-15%) for drizzle overnight \nwill subside with the progression of the trough further eastward \ninto the morning tomorrow.\n\nAs mid-level ridging and surface high pressure settles over the \nregion, temperatures will continue to remain cooler across the \nforecast area, with high temperatures in the low 50s/upper 40s \nduring the daytime tomorrow. Residual low cloud cover could serve to \nkeep temperatures cooler than forecast during the daytime, with \nfurther reinforcement of cold air from north/northwesterly winds \nthrough the vertical profile prompting greater CAA. The persistence \nof this cloud cover tomorrow evening into Saturday morning could \ndictate how low temperatures are able to fall, which has some \nimpact on the potential for frost/freeze. As of now, guidance \nstill favors temperatures falling at/below frost criteria \nareawide, with more focused chances for below freezing \ntemperatures generally east of US- 63 (50-70%). However, with \ngreater cloud cover, efficient radiational cooling will be \ninhibited, which would keep overnight lows higher than forecast.\nOtherwise, with cooler temperatures and light winds Friday \nnight into Saturday morning, conditions remain favorable for the\ndevelopment of frost/freeze across the forecast area.\n\nThe continued progression of the trough towards the East Coast, \nalongside another trough over the West Coast digging towards western \nTX, the pattern looks to deamplify over the weekend. Surface high \npressure sliding off towards the east will shift winds out of the \nsouth/southwest in the low levels, and clear skies will help \ncontribute to a warm up of temperatures back into the 60s by the \nstart of next week, with lows warming back into the 40s. The \natmospheric flow remains quasi-zonal through the early half of next \nweek, before a mid-level ridge begins to amplify across the western \nhalf of the CONUS and a trough axis digs down from the northern \nPlains by Wednesday. Aside from this shift in the synoptic pattern, \nthe remainder of the forecast looks to remain warm and dry.",
